Chapter 1

Generative Model and Probabilistic Semantics

We construct a probabilistic graphical model that captures the relationship between an unobserved world state, an external observable stream, an internal experiential state, and a subjective report. The model is intentionally minimal; all its structural assumptions are flagged for empirical verification.

Let  (\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mathbb{P})  be a probability space. All random variables are defined on this space and take values in complete separable metric (Polish) spaces.

·  W \in \mathcal{W} : the latent world state, distributed according to a prior  \mu_W .
·  O \in \mathcal{O} : the observable stream (sensory data, text, measurements).
·  E \in \mathcal{E} : the latent experiential state.
·  R \in \mathcal{R} : the subjective report, a linguistic or numeric signal reflecting the experiential state.

The joint distribution factorizes as

P(W, E, R, O) = P(W) \, P(E \mid W) \, P(R \mid E) \, P(O \mid W).
\tag{1}

The factorization encodes the following conditional independence assertions:

O \perp\!\!\!\perp R \mid W, \qquad R \perp\!\!\!\perp O \mid E, \qquad E \perp\!\!\!\perp O \mid W.

The first is the crucial structural claim: a report carries no information about the observable beyond what the world state already explains. If this independence fails in a dataset—because, for instance, the human observer had access to the outcome when reporting—the model’s central inference engine collapses. Hence, experimental protocol must collect the report before the outcome is known, or independently of the measurement stream.

The prior  P(W)  and the conditional distributions  P(E \mid W), P(R \mid E), P(O \mid W)  are assumed to belong to parametric families  \{P_\theta\}  and  \{P_\phi\}  whose precise architecture will be specified in Chapter 9.

