Chapter 2

The Qualia Likelihood Function: Definition and Variational Bound

Definition 2.1 (Qualia Likelihood Function).
For a fixed world state  w \in \mathcal{W} , the probability of observing a subjective report  R = r  is obtained by marginalising the experiential variable:

\mathcal{L}_Q(r; w) := P(R = r \mid W = w) = \int_{\mathcal{E}} P(r \mid e) \, P(e \mid w) \, \mathrm{d}e.
\tag{2}

The integral is typically intractable. We therefore seek a tractable lower bound.

Theorem 2.1 (Evidence Lower Bound for the Qualia Likelihood).
Let  q_\phi(e \mid r)  be any probability density on  \mathcal{E}  absolutely continuous with respect to  P(e \mid w) . Then

\log \mathcal{L}_Q(r; w) \ge \mathbb{E}_{q_\phi(e \mid r)}\bigl[ \log P(r \mid e) \bigr] - D_{\mathrm{KL}}\bigl( q_\phi(e \mid r) \,\|\, P(e \mid w) \bigr) =: \operatorname{ELBO}(\phi; w, r),
\tag{3}

with equality if and only if  q_\phi(e \mid r) = P(e \mid r, w)  almost everywhere.

Proof.
Start from the definition:

\log \mathcal{L}_Q(r; w) = \log \int_{\mathcal{E}} P(r \mid e) P(e \mid w) \, \mathrm{d}e.

Introduce  q_\phi(e \mid r)  inside the integral:

\log \mathcal{L}_Q(r; w) = \log \int_{\mathcal{E}} \frac{P(r \mid e) P(e \mid w)}{q_\phi(e \mid r)} \, q_\phi(e \mid r) \, \mathrm{d}e.

The integral is the expectation of the ratio under  q_\phi . By Jensen’s inequality, since the logarithm is concave,

\log \mathbb{E}_{q_\phi}\!\left[ \frac{P(r \mid e) P(e \mid w)}{q_\phi(e \mid r)} \right] \ge \mathbb{E}_{q_\phi}\!\left[ \log\!\left( \frac{P(r \mid e) P(e \mid w)}{q_\phi(e \mid r)} \right) \right].

The right-hand side expands to

\mathbb{E}_{q_\phi}\bigl[ \log P(r \mid e) \bigr] + \mathbb{E}_{q_\phi}\bigl[ \log P(e \mid w) - \log q_\phi(e \mid r) \bigr],

which is exactly  \mathbb{E}_{q_\phi}[\log P(r \mid e)] - D_{\mathrm{KL}}(q_\phi \| P(e \mid w)) .

Jensen’s inequality is an equality if and only if the integrand  \frac{P(r \mid e) P(e \mid w)}{q_\phi(e \mid r)}  is constant almost everywhere under  q_\phi . This condition implies  q_\phi(e \mid r) \propto P(r \mid e) P(e \mid w) , which, by Bayes’ rule, is the true posterior  P(e \mid r, w) . ∎

Corollary 2.1 (Jensen Gap and Pinsker Bound).
The gap between the exact log-likelihood and the ELBO is exactly the Kullback–Leibler divergence from the variational posterior to the true posterior:

\log \mathcal{L}_Q(r; w) - \operatorname{ELBO}(\phi; w, r) = D_{\mathrm{KL}}\bigl( q_\phi(e \mid r) \,\|\, P(e \mid r, w) \bigr).
\tag{4}

Applying Pinsker’s inequality yields a bound in terms of total variation distance:

D_{\mathrm{KL}}(q_\phi \| P) \ge \frac{1}{2} \| q_\phi - P \|_{\mathrm{TV}}^2.

Thus a small ELBO gap guarantees closeness of the variational approximation to the true posterior in total variation.

