Chapter 3

Augmented Posterior and Amortised Inference

The standard Bayesian posterior over world states, using only observations, is

P(w \mid O) \propto P(O \mid w) \, \mu_W(w).

With a subjective report, the augmented posterior becomes

P(w \mid O, R) \propto P(O \mid w) \, \mathcal{L}_Q(R; w) \, \mu_W(w).
\tag{5}

Because  \mathcal{L}_Q(R; w)  is intractable, we replace it by its variational approximation, yielding

\widetilde{P}(w \mid O, R) \propto P(O \mid w) \, \exp\!\bigl( \operatorname{ELBO}(\phi^*; w, R) \bigr) \, \mu_W(w),
\tag{6}

where  \phi^*  optimises the bound for each  w . Two strategies exist:

1. Pointwise optimisation: For each candidate  w  (e.g., in a particle filter), solve
   \phi^*(w) = \arg\max_\phi \operatorname{ELBO}(\phi; w, R).
   \]  
   This is computationally prohibitive.
2. Amortised inference: Train a single recognition network  \phi  to perform well over the prior predictive distribution. Formally,

\phi^* = \arg\max_\phi \; \mathbb{E}_{W \sim \mu_W}\!\bigl[ \operatorname{ELBO}(\phi; W, R) \bigr].
\tag{7}

This amortised scheme corresponds to the standard VAE paradigm, with the world state acting as an additional conditioning variable. It is the approach we adopt and implement in the architecture.

