Chapter 4

Augmented Training Objective for Language Models

Consider a language model with parameters  \theta  trained on a corpus via the standard autoregressive loss:

\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{LM}}(\theta) = -\sum_{t} \log P_\theta(x_t \mid x_{<t}).

Assume we possess a dataset  \mathcal{D}_Q = \{(O_i, R_i, y_i)\}_{i=1}^N , where  y_i  is a downstream ground-truth label. We propose to augment the language model objective with two additional terms:

\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{RQA}}(\theta, \phi) = \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{LM}}(\theta)
+ \lambda \, \mathbb{E}_{(O,R,y) \sim \mathcal{D}_Q}\!\bigl[ -\operatorname{ELBO}(\phi,\theta; O,R) \bigr]
+ \mu \, \mathbb{E}_{(O,R,y) \sim \mathcal{D}_Q}\!\bigl[ -\log P_\theta(y \mid O,R) \bigr],
\tag{8}

where  \lambda, \mu > 0  are hyperparameters. Minimising  -\operatorname{ELBO}  drives the model to learn a latent experiential space that both generates the reports faithfully and stays close to the prior  P_\theta(E \mid O) . Minimising the negative log-likelihood of  y  encourages the model to use the inferred experiential state for better world prediction.

To compute gradients, we specify the reparameterisation. Let  \varepsilon \sim p(\varepsilon)  be a noise variable, and define the encoding map  E = g_\phi(\varepsilon, R)  such that the distribution of  E  equals  q_\phi(E \mid R) . Then the gradient of the ELBO term w.r.t.  \phi  is

\nabla_\phi \mathbb{E}_{q_\phi}[\log P_\theta(R \mid E)] = \mathbb{E}_{p(\varepsilon)}\!\bigl[ \nabla_\phi \log P_\theta(R \mid g_\phi(\varepsilon,R)) \bigr].

The gradient w.r.t.  \theta  expands as

\begin{aligned}
\nabla_\theta \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{RQA}} &= \nabla_\theta \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{LM}} \\
&\quad + \lambda \, \mathbb{E}_{(O,R,y),\, q_\phi}\!\bigl[ -\nabla_\theta \log P_\theta(R \mid E) + \nabla_\theta \log P_\theta(E \mid O) \bigr] \\
&\quad + \mu \, \mathbb{E}_{(O,R,y)}\!\bigl[ -\nabla_\theta \log P_\theta(y \mid O,R) \bigr].
\end{aligned}
\tag{9}

All expectations are approximated by Monte Carlo using mini-batches. The three terms are simply cross-entropy gradients and can be computed in a single forward-backward pass.

