Chapter 5

Convergence of the Reflective Bayesian Loop

The core dynamical system of RQA is the reflective loop: an agent holds a belief  P  over world states, introspects to produce a report distributed according to its current belief, and then updates its belief by treating that report as new evidence. We now analyse the fixed-point behaviour of this loop.

Let  \mathcal{B}  denote the space of probability measures on  \mathcal{W}  with finite second moments, equipped with the Wasserstein-2 distance  d_W . A belief state is an element  P \in \mathcal{B} .

Report generation from a belief.
Under belief  P , the distribution over reports is

P_R(\cdot \mid P) = \int_{\mathcal{W}} \left( \int_{\mathcal{E}} P(R \mid E) \, P(E \mid w) \, \mathrm{d}E \right) \mathrm{d}P(w).

The report is stochastic; it is not a deterministic function of  P .

Definition 5.1 (Mean Reflection Operator).
The expected posterior after incorporating a report generated from belief  P  is

\overline{F}(P)(w) := \mathbb{E}_{R \sim P_R(\cdot \mid P)}\!\bigl[ P(w \mid O, R) \bigr],
\tag{10}

where  P(w \mid O, R)  is the Bayesian update using the true (intractable) likelihood. In practice we use the variational approximation, but the analysis holds for the true posterior.

Lipschitz properties.
We require two Lipschitz constants.

· Report sensitivity: There exists  L_R \ge 0  such that for all  P_1, P_2 \in \mathcal{B} ,
  W_1\!\bigl( P_R(\cdot \mid P_1),\, P_R(\cdot \mid P_2) \bigr) \le L_R \, d_W(P_1, P_2),
  \tag{11}
  where  W_1  is the Wasserstein-1 distance.
· Bayesian update sensitivity: For a fixed observation  O , the map  R \mapsto P(\cdot \mid O, R)  is locally Lipschitz with a constant that depends on the current belief. There exists a constant  C > 0  and a function  Z(P) = \int_{\mathcal{W}} P(O \mid w) \, \mathrm{d}P(w)  such that
  d_W\!\bigl( P(\cdot \mid O, r_1),\, P(\cdot \mid O, r_2) \bigr) \le \frac{C}{Z(P)} \, d_{\mathcal{R}}(r_1, r_2)
  \tag{12}
  for all  r_1, r_2 \in \mathcal{R} . This bound reflects the evidence-dependence: as the posterior concentrates,  Z(P)  becomes small and the posterior becomes highly sensitive to perturbations in the report.

Theorem 5.1 (Reflective Loop Convergence).
Assume there exists a compact subset  \mathcal{B}_\epsilon \subset \mathcal{B}  on which  Z(P) \ge \epsilon > 0  and such that

\kappa := L_R \cdot \sup_{P \in \mathcal{B}_\epsilon} \frac{C}{Z(P)} < 1.
\tag{13}

Then  \overline{F}  is a strict contraction on  \mathcal{B}_\epsilon . By the Banach fixed-point theorem, there exists a unique fixed point  B^* \in \mathcal{B}_\epsilon  satisfying  \overline{F}(B^*) = B^* .

Proof.
Take any  P_1, P_2 \in \mathcal{B}_\epsilon . Let  (R_1, R_2)  be a coupling of the report distributions that achieves the Wasserstein-1 distance, i.e.,

\mathbb{E}\bigl[ d_{\mathcal{R}}(R_1, R_2) \bigr] = W_1\bigl( P_R(\cdot \mid P_1),\, P_R(\cdot \mid P_2) \bigr).

Then

\begin{aligned}
d_W\!\bigl( \overline{F}(P_1), \overline{F}(P_2) \bigr)
&= d_W\!\Bigl( \mathbb{E}_{R_1}\bigl[ P(\cdot \mid O,R_1) \bigr],\; \mathbb{E}_{R_2}\bigl[ P(\cdot \mid O,R_2) \bigr] \Bigr) \\
&\le \mathbb{E}\Bigl[ d_W\!\bigl( P(\cdot \mid O,R_1),\, P(\cdot \mid O,R_2) \bigr) \Bigr] \\
&\le \sup_{P \in \mathcal{B}_\epsilon} \frac{C}{Z(P)} \; \mathbb{E}\bigl[ d_{\mathcal{R}}(R_1, R_2) \bigr] \\
&= \sup_{P \in \mathcal{B}_\epsilon} \frac{C}{Z(P)} \; W_1\bigl( P_R(\cdot \mid P_1),\, P_R(\cdot \mid P_2) \bigr) \\
&\le \kappa \, d_W(P_1, P_2).
\end{aligned}

The first inequality uses the convexity of  d_W  and Jensen’s inequality. The second applies the Lipschitz bound (12). The third uses the Lipschitz property of the report generator (11). Since  \kappa < 1 ,  \overline{F}  is a contraction, and the Banach theorem yields the unique fixed point. ∎

Interpretation of the contraction condition.
The product  \kappa = L_R \cdot \sup\, L_B(P)  must be less than one. This imposes a damping requirement: the sensitivity of the report to belief changes multiplied by the sensitivity of the belief update to report changes must be below unity. When the evidence  Z(P)  is small (near-certainty), the Bayesian update becomes extremely sharp, violating the condition. Consequently, the fixed point can only exist in regions of non-degenerate uncertainty.

