Chapter 6

The Rumination Theorem: Pathological Introspection

When the contraction condition fails globally, the reflective loop can diverge or oscillate—a mathematical signature of ruminative cognition.

Definition 6.1 (Reflective Divergence).
An orbit  \{B^{(n)}\}_{n=0}^\infty  generated by  B^{(n)} = \overline{F}^n(B^{(0)})  diverges if it is not Cauchy in  (\mathcal{B}, d_W) .

Theorem 6.1 (Rumination).
Suppose that for every compact  \mathcal{B}_\epsilon \subset \mathcal{B} ,

L_R \cdot \sup_{P \in \mathcal{B}_\epsilon} \frac{C}{Z(P)} \ge 1.
\tag{14}

Then there exist initial beliefs  B^{(0)}_1, B^{(0)}_2  such that  d_W( B^{(n)}_1, B^{(n)}_2 )  does not converge to zero. If, additionally, the inequality is strict on an open set, the map exhibits sensitive dependence on initial conditions.

Proof sketch.
If the Lipschitz constant of  \overline{F}  exceeds 1 on some region, then  \overline{F}  is not a non-expansive map and its iterates can stretch distances. The existence of separating orbits follows from standard results in the theory of non-contractive iterated function systems. A rigorous proof can be constructed via a Lyapunov exponent argument or by constructing explicit counterexamples in a finite-dimensional approximation of  \mathcal{B} . ∎

This theorem formalises the clinical observation that some introspective loops amplify uncertainty. The product  \kappa = L_R \cdot L_B  serves as a diagnostic parameter: when  \kappa < 1 , introspection settles into a stable self-consistent belief; when  \kappa > 1 , introspection generates rumination.

