Chapter 7

Hierarchical Self-Modelling and Geometric Convergence

The agent can also model its own modelling process. We define a hierarchy of models  M_0, M_1, M_2, \dots  where  M_k  is a model of the world and of the model at level  k-1 . This recursion is captured by an operator acting on a Hilbert space of conditional distributions.

Let  \mathcal{M} = L^2(\mathcal{W} \times \mathcal{R} \to [0,1]) , the space of square-integrable conditional probabilities of world state given report, with the inner product

\langle f, g \rangle = \int_{\mathcal{W} \times \mathcal{R}} f(w,r) \, g(w,r) \, \mathrm{d}w \, \mathrm{d}r.

Definition 7.1 (Self-Modelling Operator).
For a learning rate  \eta \in (0,2) , define  \Psi : \mathcal{M} \to \mathcal{M}  by

\Psi(M)(w,r) = M(w,r) + \eta \bigl( P(w \mid O, r) - M(w,r) \bigr),
\tag{15}

where  P(w \mid O, r)  is the true posterior (oracle) that the model attempts to approximate. The term  \varepsilon(w,r) = P(w \mid O,r) - M(w,r)  is the self-prediction error.

Theorem 7.1 (Geometric Convergence of Self-Modelling).
Let  M_\infty = P(\cdot \mid O,\cdot) . Then for any initial  M_0 ,

\| M_{n} - M_\infty \|_{L^2} = |1-\eta|^n \, \| M_0 - M_\infty \|_{L^2}.
\tag{16}

Proof.
By definition,

M_{k+1} - M_\infty = M_k + \eta (M_\infty - M_k) - M_\infty = (1-\eta)(M_k - M_\infty).

Taking the  L^2 -norm gives the result. ∎

The contraction constant is  |1-\eta| . Thus,  \eta=1  achieves one-step convergence to the true posterior—the standard Bayesian update. For  \eta<1 , the model converges geometrically but smoothly, which is advantageous when the true posterior is noisy or the observations are unreliable. Self-modelling, in this interpretation, is a damped Bayesian update.

