Chapter 9

Decision-Theoretic Reflection Criterion

The agent must decide whether to incur the cost of introspection. We formulate this as a decision problem.

Let  U(a, w)  be a utility function. The agent’s current belief (without reflection) is  P_0 = P(\cdot \mid O) . The optimal policy under  P_0  is

\pi_0 = \arg\max_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \mathbb{E}_{w \sim P_0}[U(a,w)].

If the agent reflects and obtains report  R , the posterior updates to  P_R = P(\cdot \mid O, R) , and the optimal policy becomes

\pi_R = \arg\max_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \mathbb{E}_{w \sim P_R}[U(a,w)].

Definition 9.1 (Expected Value of Qualia Information).
The expected gain in utility from incorporating the report is

\operatorname{EVQI} = \mathbb{E}_{R \sim P_R(\cdot \mid P_0)}\!\Bigl[ \mathbb{E}_{w \sim P_R}[U(\pi_R, w)] \Bigr] - \mathbb{E}_{w \sim P_0}[U(\pi_0, w)].
\tag{19}

Theorem 9.1 (Non-negativity of EVQI).
 \operatorname{EVQI} \ge 0 .

Proof.
For any fixed  R , because  \pi_R  maximises the expected utility under  P_R ,

\mathbb{E}_{P_R}[U(\pi_R, w)] \ge \mathbb{E}_{P_R}[U(\pi_0, w)].

Taking the expectation over  R ,

\mathbb{E}_{R}\bigl[ \mathbb{E}_{P_R}[U(\pi_R, w)] \bigr] \ge \mathbb{E}_{R}\bigl[ \mathbb{E}_{P_R}[U(\pi_0, w)] \bigr].

By the law of total expectation, the right-hand side equals  \mathbb{E}_{P_0}[U(\pi_0, w)] . Hence the difference is non-negative. ∎

The reflection decision rule is thus:

\text{reflect if } \operatorname{EVQI} > C_{\text{refl}} + C_{\text{qualia}},

where  C_{\text{refl}}  is the cognitive cost of engaging the reflective loop and  C_{\text{qualia}}  is the computational cost of running the recognition model  q_\phi . When the expected information value fails to exceed the costs, acting under the current observation-only posterior is optimal.

