Chapter 10

Information Monotonicity and the Falsification Condition

The entire RQA framework hinges on whether subjective reports carry additional predictive power beyond the observable data.

Theorem 10.1 (Information Monotonicity).
The conditional entropy of the world state given both observations and report is less than or equal to the entropy given observations alone:

H(W \mid O, R) = H(W \mid O) - I(W ; R \mid O) \le H(W \mid O).
\tag{20}

Equality holds if and only if  I(W ; R \mid O) = 0 .

Proof.
This is a direct consequence of the chain rule for mutual information:  H(W \mid O, R) = H(W \mid O) - I(W ; R \mid O) . Mutual information is non-negative. ∎

The quantity  I(W ; R \mid O)  is the mutual information between world states and subjective reports, conditioned on the observable stream. If this quantity is zero, reports add nothing; RQA reduces to a computationally expensive but useless augmentation. If it is positive, then there exist domains where human phenomenological reports genuinely enhance inference. This provides a clean falsification criterion: the central empirical hypothesis is

\mathcal{H}_1: \; I(W ; R \mid O) > 0.

The next chapter designs the benchmark to test this hypothesis.

