Chapter 11

Architecture and Technical Specification

The RQA system is implemented as a neural architecture comprising four modules.

11.1 World Encoder  f_{\theta_W} .
A Transformer encoder maps the observation sequence  O  into a fixed-dimensional vector  h_O \in \mathbb{R}^{d_W} . This vector parameterises the likelihood  P_\theta(O \mid W)  and serves as conditioning for the experiential prior  P_\theta(E \mid O) .

11.2 Qualia Encoder  q_\phi(E \mid R) .
A bidirectional Transformer with a latent attention bottleneck encodes the tokenised report  R  into the mean and diagonal covariance of a Gaussian distribution over the experiential manifold  \mathcal{E} \cong \mathbb{R}^{d_E} . The dimension  d_E  is a hyperparameter, typically set to 256. The encoder is trained to maximise the ELBO.

11.3 Qualia Decoder  P_\theta(R \mid E) .
An autoregressive Transformer decodes samples  e \in \mathcal{E}  back into natural language reports. This component is essential for the gradient signal flowing from the reconstruction term  \nabla_\theta \log P_\theta(R \mid E) .

11.4 World Predictor.
A multi-layer perceptron head attached to the concatenation of  h_O  and the encoded report (or the sampled  e ) outputs the prediction  \hat{y} . The final loss includes the cross-entropy term weighted by  \mu .

11.5 Experiential Manifold Geometry.
The space  \mathcal{E}  is modelled as a Riemannian manifold with a learnable metric tensor  g_\psi(e) . In practice, we initialise with a flat Euclidean metric and train a small network  \psi  to output a local distance scaling factor. During training, we enforce a triplet loss: for similar reports, geodesic distances (approximated by integrating  g_\psi  along the straight line) are small; for dissimilar reports, they are large. This allows the manifold to develop curvature that reflects qualitative experiential boundaries.

11.6 Reflective Loop Implementation.
At inference, the belief over  W  is maintained as a particle set  \{w^{(j)}\}_{j=1}^M . Each particle is associated with a weight. The system proceeds as follows:

1. Sample a particle  w  and generate a synthetic report  \tilde{R}  via  E \sim P_\theta(E \mid w)  and  \tilde{R} \sim P_\theta(R \mid E) .
2. The real human report  R  is encoded via  q_\phi , and the ELBO  \operatorname{ELBO}(\phi; w, R)  is computed.
3. Particle weights are multiplied by  \exp(\operatorname{ELBO})  and renormalised.
4. The loop repeats until the effective sample size stabilises.

11.7 Training Protocol.
The model is pre-trained as a causal language model on a large general corpus. Fine-tuning is performed on the curated dataset  \mathcal{D}_Q  using the RQA objective (8) with  \lambda = 0.1, \mu = 1.0 . Optimisation uses AdamW with learning rate  10^{-4}  and batch size 64. To prevent posterior collapse, we apply KL annealing: the weight on the KL divergence term in the ELBO is linearly increased from 0 to 1 over the first 10,000 steps.

