Chapter 12

Open Problems

Several mathematical and empirical challenges remain.

1. Explicit computation of the contraction constant. The inequality  L_B(P) \le C / Z(P)  requires the evidence  Z(P) = \int P(O \mid w) \mathrm{d}P(w) . In high-dimensional, deep generative models, this integral is intractable. Developing tight, computable bounds for  Z(P)  in specific likelihood families is necessary to verify the contraction condition in practice.
2. Report reliability. The model assumes  P(R \mid E)  is a faithful conditional. Human introspection is known to suffer from confabulation, post-hoc rationalisation, and cultural framing. A principled probabilistic model of report noise, perhaps via a confusion matrix between true experiential states and reported language, is currently absent.
3. Latent cultural factor identification. In the exchangeable collective posterior, the shared factor  \Theta  must be inferred jointly with the world state and individual experiences. This forms a doubly-intractable hierarchical latent variable model; variational approximations with amortised inference for  \Theta  remain an open design problem.
4. Empirical measurement of  I(W; R \mid O) . The entire theoretical apparatus stands or falls on whether subjective reports carry non-zero conditional mutual information in a real-world domain. A large-scale experiment with pre-registered protocol and rigorous statistical power analysis is the necessary next step.

