Appendix A

Wasserstein Distances and Lipschitz Constants

A.1. Definitions

Let (\mathcal{X}, d) be a Polish metric space. For p \ge 1, the Wasserstein-p distance between two probability measures \mu, \nu \in \mathcal{P}_p(\mathcal{X}) is

W_p(\mu, \nu) = \left( \inf_{\gamma \in \Gamma(\mu, \nu)} \int_{\mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{X}} d(x,y)^p \, \mathrm{d}\gamma(x,y) \right)^{1/p},

where \Gamma(\mu,\nu) is the set of all couplings of \mu and \nu. For p=1, the Kantorovich–Rubinstein dual formulation holds:

W_1(\mu,\nu) = \sup_{f \in \mathrm{Lip}_1} \left| \int_{\mathcal{X}} f \, \mathrm{d}\mu - \int_{\mathcal{X}} f \, \mathrm{d}\nu \right|,

with \mathrm{Lip}_1 the set of 1-Lipschitz functions f : \mathcal{X} \to \mathbb{R}.

A.2. Lipschitz Constant of the Bayesian Update

Sprungk (2020) and Cvetković & Lie (2025) establish that the posterior map R \mapsto P(\cdot \mid O, R) is locally Lipschitz under W_2 with a constant inversely proportional to the evidence. Concretely, for a dominated model with likelihood p(O \mid W) and a prior \mu_W,

W_2\bigl(P(\cdot \mid O, r_1), P(\cdot \mid O, r_2)\bigr) \le \frac{C}{Z(P)} \, |r_1 - r_2|,

where Z(P) = \int_{\mathcal{W}} p(O \mid w) \, \mathrm{d}P(w) is the marginal likelihood of the observation under the current belief P. The constant C depends on the smoothness of the likelihood in W and the dimension of \mathcal{W}. For Gaussian likelihoods, C can be bounded by the operator norm of the Fisher information.

A.3. Wasserstein Contraction of the Mean Reflection Operator

In the proof of Theorem 5.1, we used the inequality

d_W\!\bigl(\mathbb{E}_{R \sim P_R}[P(\cdot \mid O,R)],\, \mathbb{E}_{R' \sim Q_R}[P(\cdot \mid O,R')]\bigr) \le \mathbb{E}_{(R,R')}\bigl[ d_W(P(\cdot \mid O,R), P(\cdot \mid O,R')) \bigr],

which holds because the Wasserstein-2 distance is jointly convex in its arguments (a property of optimal transport). More explicitly, if \gamma is a coupling of the report distributions P_R and Q_R achieving W_1(P_R, Q_R), then the joint distribution over (R,R') can be lifted to a coupling of the corresponding posteriors, and the distance between the expected posteriors is bounded by the expectation of the pointwise distances.

