Appendix B

De Finetti’s Theorem and the Collective Posterior Bound

B.1. Statement of de Finetti’s Theorem

Let R_1, R_2, \dots be an infinite exchangeable sequence of random variables taking values in a Polish space. Then there exists a unique probability measure \mu on the space of probability measures \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{R}) such that for any n,

P(R_1, \dots, R_n) = \int_{\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{R})} \prod_{i=1}^n Q(R_i) \, \mathrm{d}\mu(Q).

The parameter Q is the limiting empirical distribution. In our setting, we condition on the world state W=w, so the representation holds for each w with a corresponding \mu(\cdot \mid w).

B.2. Proof of the Log-Likelihood Difference Bound

We wish to bound

\Delta = \left| \log P_{\text{naive}}(w \mid R_{1:N}, O) - \log P_{\text{corrected}}(w \mid R_{1:N}, O) \right|.

The naive posterior assumes conditional independence: P_{\text{naive}}(R_{1:N} \mid w) = \prod_i P(R_i \mid w). The corrected version under exchangeability is

P_{\text{corrected}}(R_{1:N} \mid w) = \int_{\Theta} \prod_i P(R_i \mid w, \Theta) \, \mathrm{d}\mu(\Theta \mid w).

Taking logarithms and applying the log-sum inequality,

\log P_{\text{naive}} - \log P_{\text{corrected}} = \log \prod_i P(R_i \mid w) - \log \int_{\Theta} \prod_i P(R_i \mid w, \Theta) \, \mathrm{d}\mu.

Using the representation P(R_i \mid w) = \int_{\Theta} P(R_i \mid w, \Theta) \, \mathrm{d}\mu, the difference is bounded by the total correlation among the reports given w. Precisely, by the data processing inequality and the chain rule of mutual information,

|\log P_{\text{naive}} - \log P_{\text{corrected}}| \le \sum_{i=1}^N D_{\mathrm{KL}}\bigl( P(R_i \mid w) \,\|\, P(R_i \mid w, \Theta) \bigr) \le \sum_{1 \le i < j \le N} \operatorname{MI}(R_i; R_j \mid w).

Each term is the conditional mutual information between two reports given the world state, which vanishes if and only if the reports are conditionally independent. The sum quantifies the cumulative double-counting error incurred by the naive product formula.

