Appendix C

Gradient Derivations for the RQA Objective

C.1. ELBO Gradient with Reparameterisation

Let E = g_\phi(\varepsilon, R) with \varepsilon \sim p(\varepsilon) a base distribution (e.g., standard Gaussian). The reconstruction term is

\mathcal{R}(\phi, \theta) = \mathbb{E}_{q_\phi(E \mid R)}[\log P_\theta(R \mid E)].

Under the reparameterisation,

\mathcal{R}(\phi, \theta) = \mathbb{E}_{p(\varepsilon)}[\log P_\theta(R \mid g_\phi(\varepsilon, R))].

Differentiating under the integral sign,

\nabla_\phi \mathcal{R} = \mathbb{E}_{p(\varepsilon)}[\nabla_\phi \log P_\theta(R \mid g_\phi(\varepsilon, R))].

By the chain rule, \nabla_\phi \log P_\theta(R \mid g_\phi) = (\nabla_g \log P_\theta(R \mid g)) \cdot \nabla_\phi g_\phi(\varepsilon, R). This is an unbiased stochastic gradient.

C.2. Gradient of the KL Divergence

The KL term in the ELBO is

\mathcal{K}(\phi, \theta) = D_{\mathrm{KL}}(q_\phi(E \mid R) \,\|\, P_\theta(E \mid O)).

For Gaussian q_\phi and P_\theta, this divergence has a closed-form expression. The gradient with respect to \phi can be computed analytically; with respect to \theta, it requires differentiating through the prior parameters. In our objective, the prior P_\theta(E \mid O) is parameterised by the world encoder, so \nabla_\theta \mathcal{K} contributes to the overall gradient via the chain rule through the observation encoder.

C.3. Combined Gradient

The full gradient in (9) is derived by summing the language model gradient and the ELBO gradient (with signs appropriate for minimisation of -\mathrm{ELBO}). All expectations are estimated by Monte Carlo with mini-batches of size B. The variance of the reparameterised gradient can be reduced using control variates (e.g., the REINFORCE baseline), but we find the reparameterisation alone to be sufficient for stable training.

