Appendix E

Rumination Theorem: A Rigorous Proof Sketch

We provide additional detail for Theorem 6.1.

E.1. Preliminaries

Assume  \mathcal{B}  is a complete metric space under d_W. The mean reflection operator \overline{F} is a continuous map. If the global Lipschitz constant is at least 1, then \overline{F} is not a contraction. We show that there exist orbits that do not converge.

E.2. Construction of a Divergent Pair

Suppose L_R \cdot L_B(P) \ge 1 + \epsilon on an open set \mathcal{U} \subset \mathcal{B}. Choose B^{(0)}_1, B^{(0)}_2 \in \mathcal{U} such that d_W(B^{(0)}_1, B^{(0)}_2) = \delta > 0 and the segment between them lies in \mathcal{U}. By the mean value theorem for metric spaces (if \mathcal{B} is a geodesic space), there exists a point along the geodesic where the local expansion factor exceeds 1+\epsilon/2. Then, by iterating, the distance between the orbits grows as

d_W(\overline{F}^n(B^{(0)}_1), \overline{F}^n(B^{(0)}_2)) \ge \delta \prod_{k=0}^{n-1} \ell_k,

where \ell_k \ge 1+\epsilon/2 for infinitely many k as long as the orbits stay in \mathcal{U}. Hence the sequence is not Cauchy. Sensitive dependence on initial conditions follows from the positive Lyapunov exponent along the orbit.

E.3. Consequence for Rumination

The failure of convergence implies that the belief does not settle into a stable fixed point; instead, it oscillates or diverges, consistent with the clinical definition of rumination as perseverative, non-adaptive introspection.

